<Collapsible id="wp_mat_unit" title="Unit plan">
<Markdown id="wp_mat_unit_md">
## UNIT PLAN

```
MAPLEWOOD ELEMENTARY  ·  GRADE 4  ·  ROOM 14  ·  C. BOYD
UNIT 5: INVESTIGATING MATTER
Quarter 3, Weeks 19–21 · 7 science periods · begins Tues Jan 13

STANDARDS (from kit alignment sheet, pp. ii–iii)
  4.PS1.1  matter exists in states; states have observable properties
  4.PS1.2  heating and cooling change the state of a substance
  also touches 4.ESS2.1 (frost wedging / weathering)


SCHEDULING
  45-minute science period, Mon–Thu. Friday period goes to social studies.
  Unit = 7 periods over 2.5 weeks, allowing for one loss to assembly/weather.

PLACEMENT IN THE YEAR
  Week 19 of 36. Second week back from winter break. Chosen deliberately:
  the phenomenon is a frozen lake and it is January.

STORYLINE
  L1  Frozen lake. Notice and wonder. Name the three states.        [THE LAUNCH]
  L2  Sort it. Stations: solid / liquid / gas, observable properties.
  L3  Water is strange. Jar of water in the freezer overnight —
      it expands. Ice floats. Cracked-pipe photo. Frost wedging.     &lt;-- KEY
  L4  Melting and freezing. Ice-melt race. Changes we can undo.
  L5  Where did the puddle go? Evaporation and condensation.
  L6  Back to Torch Lake. Layers under the ice. How the fish live.
  L7  Summative task.
  L3 IS THE PAYOFF LESSON. Why ice floats, why a full bottle cracks, why a
  pipe bursts, why a rock splits — all of it is L3. Park those questions in
  L1 and L2. Do not answer them early, it spends the surprise.

SUMMATIVE TASK (L7)
  "Torch Lake Explanation." Labeled cross-section of the lake in winter:
  ice layer, water below, fish. Written explanation answering the unit
  question — How are the fish still alive under the ice? Must use the words
  solid, liquid, freeze, melt, float. Rubric on back of kit card 12.
  Extension for early finishers: write four sentences telling a Memphis
  homeowner why to let a faucet drip on a freeze night.

MATERIALS / KIT
  MATTER MATTERS! A Hands-On Unit for Grades 3-5
  Ridgeline Science Kits, 2009 edition. Purchased 2009, my own money, $214.
  Complete except 3 broken thermometers and the VHS — video replaced with a
  clip off the internet.
```

