<Collapsible id="wp_mat_lesson" title="Lesson plan, Lesson 1: the launch">
<Markdown id="wp_mat_lesson_md">
## LESSON PLAN — LESSON 1, THE LAUNCH

```
LESSON 1 OF 7   ·   TUES JAN 13   ·   1:15–2:00   ·   45 MIN
Kit pp. 4–7, adapted

OBJECTIVE
  Students will observe a phenomenon and generate questions about it, and will
  name the three states of matter.
  (Written on board as: I can notice and wonder about a frozen lake. I can name
  the three states of matter.)

MATERIALS
  - Video clip, 90 sec. Frozen lake, skaters, snow on the far shore. No
    narration, no music, no text. Cued up, full screen, sound off anyway.
  - Slide deck (8 slides, flash drive) · chart paper, blue + black markers
  - Handout, half sheet, 24 copies + 3 spare · my Torch Lake photo, Xmas 1998

TIMELINE
  0–5    Lights down. Play clip. Play it twice if they are quiet. No talking
         during, they will want to talk during.
  5–10   Turn and talk: How are the fish still alive under there?
  10–20  Share out. Take 4–5. Build the NOTICE / WONDER chart as they talk.
  20–30  Slides 5–6. Three states. Freezer question. Write the three words
         on the board.
  30–40  Hand out half sheets. Exit ticket, silent, independent.
  40–45  Collect. Stack by table. Line up for specials.

ANTICIPATED STUDENT RESPONSES
  "It's cold" / "it's winter"               — take it, move on
  "The lake is frozen"                      — good, use the word solid
  "They're swimming under it"               — yes, where we want to go
  "Fish would freeze / they'd die"          — good wrong answer, chart it
  "There's a hole in it" / ice fishing      — likely, several of them fish
                                              with family
  "Why does ice float if it's water?"       — EXPECT THIS. Chart it.
                                              SAVE FOR L3. Do not answer.
  "How thick is the ice"                    — chart it
  "Have you been there Ms. Boyd"            — 20 seconds, then move
  If nobody gets to the fish: ask directly, "so what happens to the fish?"

CLASSROOM MANAGEMENT
  Keep momentum — this group tangents. Last year's launch ran 12 min over and
  we never got to the exit ticket. Cap share-out at 5 hands. If a story starts,
  thank them and take the next hand. Watch the 20-min mark.
  Diego: check at his desk, not cold-call. He freezes up in front of the room.
  [different pen: ask Mrs. R about his screening results]

EXIT TICKET
  On the half sheet. "Draw and label what you think is happening under the ice."
  Looking for: a line for the ice, water below, a fish. Labels optional today.
  Not graded — sorted into three piles for L2 grouping.
```

